# Nails by Fufs — environment template. Contains NO real credentials.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then fill in the placeholders
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Next.js loads it automatically for the app; the Prisma
# CLI loads it through prisma7.config.ts (Prisma 7 does not read .env itself).

# --- Database -------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime connection used by the application (src/lib/prisma/db.ts).
# Supabase Supavisor TRANSACTION pooler — port 6543.
#
# TLS: the certificate IS verified, and that is not controlled from this URL.
# Supabase's pooler serves a certificate from a private CA (`Supabase Root 2021
# CA` — self-signed, absent from public trust stores), so `sslmode=require` alone
# would leave the connection encrypted but unverified — vulnerable to a
# man-in-the-middle presenting its own certificate. The root is therefore pinned
# in `certs/supabase-root-2021.crt`, and `src/lib/prisma/tls.ts`:
#   * strips every ssl/libpq parameter from this URL, so nothing here can weaken
#     the decision, and
#   * connects with `{ ca, rejectUnauthorized: true }`, so the chain is validated.
# The CLI path (`npm run db:pull` / `db:validate` / `db:drift`) is rewritten to
# `sslmode=verify-full` with the same CA by `scripts/prisma-verified.mjs`.
# `uselibpqcompat=true` is kept so the URL still parses under pg's libpq
# semantics; it has no effect once the parameters are stripped.
#
# Do NOT append `pgbouncer=true`: that flag disabled prepared statements in
# Prisma's removed Rust query engine. Prisma 7 runs through the
# `@prisma/adapter-pg` driver, which does not cache prepared statements, so
# transaction mode is already safe.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?uselibpqcompat=true&sslmode=require"

# CLI-only connection used by `npm run db:pull` / `db:sync` / `db:validate`.
# Supabase Supavisor SESSION pooler — port 5432. Introspection needs a session
# connection, so this is deliberately separate from DATABASE_URL.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require"

# --- Custom-order reference images (server only) ---------------------------
# Used by src/lib/supabase/admin.ts to mint short-lived signed upload/download
# URLs for the private `custom-order-references` bucket, and to clean up
# orphaned objects.
#
# The secret key is PRIVILEGED: it bypasses Storage RLS and the table grants
# that keep custom-order submissions private. Never give it a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name,
# never import that module into a Client Component (it is marked `server-only`
# to make that a build error), and never log it.
#
# Project Settings → API keys → secret key (`sb_secret_...`). The legacy
# `service_role` JWT also works but the current-format secret key is preferred.
SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY="sb_secret_..."

# --- Admin (studio owner only) ---------------------------------------------
# Public Supabase project URL and its PUBLISHABLE (anon) key. Both are public by
# design: the browser uses them to sign the owner in. The privileged
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY above must never be given a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY="<publishable-or-anon-key>"

# Who may administer the studio. An authenticated Supabase user is ONLY treated
# as an admin when their email appears here (comma-separated). There is no public
# sign-up: create the owner's account in the Supabase dashboard
# (Authentication → Users → Add user), then list that email below.
ADMIN_EMAILS="<owner-email>"
# Optional: allow specific user ids as well (useful if the email changes).
# ADMIN_USER_IDS=""

# --- Abuse protection ------------------------------------------------------
# Salt for the rate-limit caller hash (src/lib/security/rate-limit.ts).
#
# The limiter has to tell callers apart without storing who they are: the key it
# writes to `rate_limit_counters` is SHA-256(salt + client identifier), so the
# table can never be read back as a visitor log.
#
# Set this to any long random string in production. It is safe to rotate — the
# only effect is that in-flight counters start fresh — but rotating it mid-attack
# resets the attacker's allowance too, so prefer a stable value.
#
# Locally, a fixed development fallback is used. In production it is REQUIRED:
# the application refuses rate-limited requests rather than hash with a known
# value, and a production build fails with a clear message so a forgotten
# variable cannot ship. Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 48
RATE_LIMIT_SALT="<long-random-string>"
